 {"type": "text", "text":
  "Before 1900 almost all Greek prose was written in some form of katharevousa, and the two great storytellers of the 1880s–1900s, **Alexandros Papadiamantis** and **Georgios Vizyinos**, developed a mixed style that is still the hardest and most rewarding reading in Greek: **katharevousa narration** (augmented imperfects, learned aorists, participles, dative relics) wrapped around **demotic speech, folk songs and island or Thracian dialect** in the dialogue. The switch between the two is itself meaningful: the narrator's learned voice versus the characters' living one.\n\n"
  "Three openings: *Η Φόνισσα* (1903), Papadiamantis' dark masterpiece about an old woman on Skiathos; *Όνειρο στο κύμα* (1900), his most lyrical story; and Vizyinos' *Το μόνον της ζωής του ταξείδιον* (1884), where a narrator remembers his boyhood dreams of the tailor-boy who marries a princess. Don't translate every form: use the table at the end to recognise the dozen patterns that recur, and read for the story. Glosses give the dictionary form plus, for katharevousa forms, the modern equivalent.\n\n"
  "*Πηγές: Αλέξανδρος Παπαδιαμάντης, Η Φόνισσα (1903), Όνειρο στο κύμα (1900); Γεώργιος Βιζυηνός, Το μόνον της ζωής του ταξείδιον (1884). Public domain, Greek Wikisource; polytonic originals given in monotonic.*"},
